## Unite different perspectives

Phuong: Also since the team is so spread out and you have a lot of collaborators, how are decisions made at Dinamo? Because I can imagine a lot of times decision making involves a compromise and maybe no one is happy with the compromise?

Fabian: Very good question. I don't think there's one way how we always solve and decide things. I think it's very situation-specific, set-up-specific, who is involved, what is the project, and so on? For me personally, working together in a team, having different voices always feels more exciting always. I probably couldnt even do any of it alone? Of course there is stuff that I have clear personal preferences about – and sometimes they are different from the preference of other team members. But zoomed out, I find it most exciting to see where something becomes equally interesting to everybody involved. Kind of letting the process and the different perspectives guide you to a point where you say “this is what everybody involved finds exciting”. Of course you could call that “a compromise”. But in this moment I don't see a compromise as a bad thing or as non-quality. On the contrary, I think it's super nice because it is kind of the result and the consequence of these people, of this situation. And if you had a different situation, a different team, then you would also very naturally end up with something else. Especially if you do this over a long time, it stays exciting because you don't repeat yourself. Plus it feels way less influential what other people outside of the team think about it? Even if somebody else will hate it, it somehow does not matter much because we all in the team like it together?

Mira: Is there a difference in custom typefaces – that the clients influence more the decisions or are you more or less free in what you do and they just trust you? How is it usually?

Fabian: Of course, clients have quite a big say in what you do and what a project becomes. We sometimes have people or companies that come to us and give us a lot of freedom, that give us almost a carte blanche to design something for them. But most of the time they come some sort a “problem”: a technical requirement, a specific function the type should fulfil for them, and also often they have time or budget restrictions of course. But since you can start with a pre-existing typeface for a custom font and adjust it, usually there is a large range of possibilities and you can almost always find a way to make sense of a brief. If the timeline is short you can only do changes that do not require a lot of testing and development. If the budget is small, you can organise a customisation in phases and extend and update a design once they have more money at hand again, and so on? Sometimes these restrictions can be a bit of a challenge and you wish they would be different to reach the fullest potential of an idea. But most of the time we are quite good in finding something that is exciting and interesting for us to be doing, despite any odds. In that perspective, limitations can be quite helpful and inspiring as well?

Mira: They give a structure you can work in.

Fabian: Totally! I always love when a specific framework is leading you to something that you wouldn’t do in another framework. But of course you something find something exciting for yourselves but the client is not sharing the perspective. Sometimes that can feel a bit frustrating. Yet the nice thing with these kinds of ideas is that you can keep them in mind and often test or use them again later, on other projects. We had this a couple of times already, that a client did not like an idea and then we just did it anyways for our own library.

## Becoming a team

Phuong:  From the way you described, it seems that communication is very organic. It's very communicative how decisions are made, how you give feedback. But I think it's not really easy to do that with many people. How did you find the people that are open to that? Because normally if you just look at somebody's work, you don't know the way they work with others, right?

Fabian: Absolutely! For us, a lot of this happened and keeps happening quite organically. A super powerful thing for us was having interns quite early. Almost learning things together, you develop a really shared approach to things and very compatible workflows. And then after an internship you can super easily come together again to work on projects together again. Or sometimes internships even ended in permanent setups and you keep working together. And connections often “snowball” a bit and one person that you already worked with, and that was already great to work with, links you with another person that is also great and you start to work together as well. But it always takes times to get to know each other and find out of course. With more experience you definitely grow a better gut feeling. But it also happened already that we see the work of somebody and it resonates and feels close to your own interests, but when you meet the person you realise they work in very different ways from your own processes and that you wouldn't be compatible to really work together side by side and very closely? Because we already have a quite big and well tuned setup and team. So, everybody new needs to fit with all that is already there, right!

Phuong: You probably never fully know until you really take the chance and work with somebody.

Fabian: Completely. Sometimes you can get some insights if you know somebody that already worked with a potential collaborator previously. Especially if you have to find somebody swift, I always love this “word of mouth” recommendations or endorsements the most!

Phuong: Yeah, totally. I'm just curious, and this is a bit nosy, have there been examples of things that went surprisingly good or surprisingly bad from your experience?

