## What is Collaboration?

Mira: Maybe we can take one step back at this point. After these talking points, we would love to know what you understand under the term collaboration. Because we talked a lot about working together, working with others, working in a team, collaborating with externals or …

Phuong: … to co-create.

Mira: Looking at setups can be simple and practical. But sometimes it can also get quite philosophical and personal.  What does collaborating, doing a collaboration mean for you exactly?

Phuong: Discussing between us, we already had differing opinions from each other.

Mira: So we thought it would be super interesting to ask you as well.

Fabian: I agree, the word can mean a lot of things depending on the context in which it is used in? In fashion for example where you have a lot of product collaborations between brands or designers. For me personally, when I say “collaborating” I really mean working together on eye level. Bringing ideas to the table together and finding the best way to create something together, that is “ours” afterwards. Different and more than if each of us would have done it alone? In a way, it is bit what we already talked about before. This positive kind of “compromise". Again, calling this a “compromise” quickly sounds a bit weird, because has often a negative association. But what I mean is more the “going steps towards each other”, somewhere new and different from where you’d automatically and normally would go when working on something alone?

Phuong: Maybe a better word to describe it could be “negotiation"?

Fabian: It’s tricky, right? Its different from your autopilot, so its not “fully your personal preference”. But you still want to go there, because you know its even more exciting there? And you really need the other’s contribution as you wouldn’t get there by yourself alone?

Mira: Yeah, there is quickly a negative sound to it, even though its actually something positive.

Fabian: Maybe we need to find or create a new word to fully cover all these aspects?

Mira: At least in “compromise" there is this “co”. Something from “both”. Which I think is the nicest aspect about it. I mean, when a compromise leads to a decision that all parties aren't happy anymore, then it also is not really a compromise – then it's more a bad decision?

Fabian: This is a nice way to look at it.

