## It takes a village

Phuong: On that note, what is the project that involves the most people in Dinamo?

Fabian: Everything Dinamo does involves quite a lot of people by now. If you design a typeface that goes to the library, that probably touches everybody that is part of Dinamo at some point in time. Some of us work on the idea for the design, more people get involved during the development and the the production, then there is engineering, kerning, mastering. Afterwards some of us are creating a campaign, to accompanying it when it gets launched. And afterwards some of us are involved in licensing the design, or to support it when people have troubles installing or embedding it or so? And if all goes full circle, maybe there will even be requests coming in inquiring for a custom versions of the design and then somebody creates an estimate and sets up a service agreement, and then and then the process starts anew again with the type designers adding tweaks to the typeface, before handing it over to the engineers who do the re-mastering and so on? But of course this all is happening over long time stretches. It's not everybody working together on one project at the same time – it's more phases.

Phuong: We saw on the website, under every typeface, there is always a long list of collaborators that are credited. That's why we're curious – is there one that has a very long list?

Fabian: The longest list by now is probably the one of Diatype. I think Diatype has 8 or 9 different writing systems that it supports. So that is a lot of people that worked on it.

Mira: I think even 10.

Fabian: Maybe even 10? It really is large by now.

Mira: We also saw in the credits that everyone is having their kind of role in this process.

Fabian: Absolutely. Most of us at Dinamo do a few things next to each other. But of course, everybody has a bit of their specialties. Some work on the design of the typefaces, while others work on the design of the campaigns to release and communicate the typefaces. Or there is people in our team that know to design multiple writing systems, and then that becomes a bit their “authorities”. Olga, Jovana and Dasha for example design Cyrillics as well. Or Mingoo is our only designer that knows Hangul. Panos or Jovana can also draw Greeks. Or Wael, KJ and Lana work with us on Arabics. Some of them work on a fixed basis with us, but others are more project based. Because a lot of these alphabets take way more work and time to be created than a Latin alphabet. So, often these writing systems are a bit depending on bigger clients requesting and cross-financing their development.

Phuong: Is that why Diatype has the largest language support? Because I assume it's also the most popular typeface in your catalogue?

Fabian: Absolutely. Diatype is definitely the most low key design in our library. One of those typefaces that always works, because its design is mega neutral. Personally, I always prefer fonts that are doing something a bit more unexpected. So design-wise, Diatype certainly is not my most favourite Dinamo font. But since over the years we had so many requests and opportunities to extend it to support Greek and Arabic and Thai and Georgian and Devanagari and so on, that it became one of our most meaningful Dinamo project to me because it gave us the opportunity to connect with so many great people and it bears so many memories of working together on it.

Mira: It seem like super nice opportunities to me, involving other people wherever you don't have an expertise for something yourself. Maybe in typeface design this is more common and normal? But in graphic design I totally think we could learn and celebrate that more? I often feel that we are expected to know and be able to do everything that a project includes? Designing the layout, taking pictures, creating illustrations, pre-press, image lithography, managing print production, and so on. But involving other people in projects gives you new insights into your own project. I think this is something we can all learn more from each other – to integrate as many people as possible because it's nice to help each other. Now when we all write our thesis, it's so nice to get help and help others. Even across the school departments. I d help friends from other departments with their thesis layouts. And in return I would help receive help with things that I am not that knowledgeable about? You can equally benefit from it and that is so empowering. And these relationships can easily continue and grow beyond the school context later?

Fabian: Trading work for work is always super nice, I agree. But it can also quickly become more complex once you enter a more commercial setup of course. With typefaces becoming products, there often is opportunities for nice partnerships. Like for example multiple designers contributing different writing systems to a bigger typeface family, and instead of charging for their work, they later receive a royalty split from the licensing sales of the design later on. That way you not only tap into competence that you wouldn’t have yourself, but you also create more stability for each other, as afterwards you might get a small percentage of every sale of the font. And not only if that very specific writing system sells that you are able to design? But again, this isnt equally easy for all writing systems of course: A well produced Latin typeface for example might contain 700 or 800 glyphs. While a Hangul typeface might require more than 10’000 glyphs before you can properly use it. So, designing and contributing a Latin design is much more quickly doable than designing and contributing a Hangul design for a collaborative project for example? In those moment I feel it really is important that there is clear monetary solutions in exchange for the work. At Dinamo we often agree to develop larger writing systems over longer time and pay the designers in instalments that we can more easily afford. Or sometimes we are lucky and we have a bigger client that needs a larger license and then they crossfinance the development through that.

Phuong: It's also a super nice way of creating networks and community between different places and cultures. I'm originally from Vietnam and the other day I saw that you’ve been collaborating with Duc Cao for your Diatype design to add our special to diacritics. I was so excited that finally I can use Diatype to type my name with it!

Fabian: Super nice to hear. Yes, Duc is amazing! We totally have to talk to him again soon and try to make more designs in our library supporting Vietnamese. Its not that many diacritics that you have to add.

Phuong: Yes you totally should. Because surprisingly, there isnt that many typeface designs that support Vietnamese by default still. We’d love to have more options!

Fabian: That’s a great insight and motivation. We totally should.

## An advice for us

Mira: Maybe we can nicely connect this with one of the last questions that we wanted to ask: Is there any advice that you’d have for us?

Fabian: One last big question! What could be a nice thing for me to answer here?

Mira: Maybe something you wish another person would have told you?

Fabian: Thinking back for ourselves with Dinamo, what always helped us a lot was staying open and figuring things out on the go. There's so much stuff that in the beginning you dont know or you cant foresee. Still up until today, actually. So, it really doesnt make that much sense to worry but I feel its usually best to take things one step at the time? And even if things go bad sometimes, I think it's super important to not letting that stop you? It totally helps to not having to deal with things completely alone, but to have other nice people around you to go thru tough stuff together? And maybe one more thing: That you always trust yourself and your ideas? Especially today when everything is so well documented and shared and you constantly see nice work of other people, I feel it is so important to find your own angles and ways to do things?

Phuong: For our thesis, your point about flexibility certainly is relevant for us. For example at the start of the project, we actually didn't plan to do interviews. We just planned to make some experiments together but then slowly a bigger project has grown from there. If you had asked me to do interviews in the beginning of our collaboration, I probably would have said that this isnt my skills and that it makes no sense for me to do interviews. But one thing lead to the next and out of the sudden it totally felt right and exciting to get in contact and talk to people?

Fabian: I always love those moments as well. That surprise and trust that you will figure it out somehow. You don't know the answer yet – but you will find one.

